llbrand dives  in and rescues her from a watery grave  this of course is after he abandoned  several other survivors from his ship  when it was attacked by a giant sea  monster in one of the character posters  for the show paul brandt is seen holding  a sword with a horsehead pommel while it  might be easy to assume that this has  something to do with the rohirum it's  worth remembering that the horselords of  rohan don't exist at this point in  middle earth's history paul brand's  sword likely indicates he is numenorean  tolkien writes in unfinished tales that  the dunadine loved horses and riding and  became so attuned to their steeds that  they could call them to their side by  merely thinking it so there have also  been images of him wearing a tunic  marked with a solar symbol similar to  one found on another character poster  fans have speculated this icon may  belong to the numenorean noble elendil  that's as far as our knowledge of  hallbrand goes currently it'll probably  be a while before we find out who he  really is what he's running from and  what he might become  the stranger is by far the most  mysterious character in the rings of  power he arrives in middle earth via  fireball which can be seen streaking  across the sky the relationship between  the stranger and the harfoot nori who  first encounters him seems to be a  central storyline for the series the  stranger could be a wizard but he  probably isn't even if he is he almost  certainly won't be the one you're  thinking of gandalf radagast and saruman  all come to middle earth in the third  age long after the events of the show  take place the stranger might be one of  the two blue wizards but it seems odd to  introduce one with such fanfare and not  the other all things considered it's not  particularly plausible that the stranger  is one of these starry  so who is he well you might want to  consider a character you won't find  elsewhere on this list someone who is  guaranteed to play a major role in the  series one of the most famous aspects of  sauron's story during the second age is  that he comes to the elves disguised in  a form so fair that few suspect his true  identity what better way of delivering a  truly shocking twist than to make the  audience believe they're watching a  kindly wizard befriend a humble harfoot  when they are in fact witnessing the  return of the new dark lord  nobody expected to see hobbits in the  rings of power mostly because they don't  show up in middle earth's history until  the third age and yet here they are or  at least their ancestors the harfoots  like the hobbits are short and have  hairy feet but that's where the  resemblance ends the harfoots are a  nomadic group still searching to find  their place in the world the shows  featured harfoots nori brandyfoot poppy  proudfellow largo brandyfoot and  marigoldbrandyfoot encounter the  stranger on their travels which changes  their lives forever the series depiction  of this tribe of halflings is almost  entirely lifted from concerning hobbits  the first chapter of the lord of the  rings this prologue explains that in the  so-called wandering days hobbits  inhabited the upper veils of anduin to  the east of the misty mountains  according to tolkien the harfots had  much to do with dwarves in ancient times  and long lived in the foothills of the  mountains only when the fortress of dol  guldur rose in the early third age did  the hobbits migrate westwards eventually  settling in the lands that became the  shire clearly these aren't the simple  merry gardeners we all know and love but  the harford's tale should nevertheless  offer up a tantalizing opportunity to  explore the secret history of one of  middle earth's most remarkable races  i can't help but feel there's  wonders in this world  beyond our wondering  because of the show's condensed timeline  we can assume the movers and shakers of  late numenorean history will be present  from early on including isildur son of  