andi's  watchtower reveals a lot about this land  the geography of the valley below bears  a striking resemblance to the river and  duin the hills of emin arnon and the  ethel duoth mountain range that makes up  mordor's western border this could mean  much of render's season 1 story takes  place in and around the forest later  known as ethilian the perfect home for a  lovesick wood elf  an inhabitant of tyrod bronwyn is a  healer and a single mother to son theo  by episode 2 there is still little else  known about the woman who loves iran  dear however she quickly proves her  resourcefulness and determination when  she saves theo from a goblin that has  burrowed up from beneath her house this  occurs shortly after she returns from  her hometown which has been destroyed  under mysterious circumstances using the  severed head of the goblin she convinces  her fellow villagers to abandon their  homes in the event that tirarod suffers  the same fate early in the series theo  discovers a broken sword hidden in the  floorboards of a barn the weapon is  currently a total mystery for a while  speculation ran rampant that it could be  gurthang the sword of turin tarambar  however wright's restrictions prevent  amazon from using material found solely  in the silmarillion now that we can  watch the full episodes and have gotten  a good look at the design of the sword's  hilt and pommel and sauron's mark on the  blade this proves that it's definitively  a weapon of the dark lord it's clear  that theo has some kind of connection to  the wider events of the second age and  that the tirarod storyline will reach  far beyond the secret romance at its  core another character created for the  rings of power hallbrand is a mortal man  running from his past by episode 2 we  still know little about the mysterious  figure though he clearly has some  compassion as hallbrand and galadriel  attempt to survive on a makeshift raft  the elf is dragged under the waves  during a powerful storm hallbrand dives  in and rescues her from a watery grave  this of course is after he abandoned  several other survivors from his ship  when it was attacked by a giant sea  monster in one of the character posters  for the show paul brandt is seen holding  a sword with a horsehead pommel while it  might be easy to assume that this has  something to do with the rohirum it's  worth remembering that the horselords of  rohan don't exist at this point in  middle earth's history paul brand's  sword likely indicates he is numenorean  tolkien writes in unfinished tales that  the dunadine loved horses and riding and  became so attuned to their steeds that  they could call them to their side by  merely thinking it so there have also  been images of him wearing a tunic  marked with a solar symbol similar to  one found on another character poster  fans have speculated this icon may  belong to the numenorean noble elendil  that's as far as our knowledge of  hallbrand goes currently it'll probably  be a while before we find out who he  really is what he's running from and  what he might become  the stranger is by far the most  mysterious character in the rings of  power he arrives in middle earth via  fireball which can be seen streaking  across the sky the relationship between  the stranger and the harfoot nori who  first encounters him seems to be a  central storyline for the series the  stranger could be a wizard but he  probably isn't even if he is he almost  certainly won't be the one you're  thinking of gandalf radagast and saruman  all come to middle earth in the third  age long after the events of the show  take place the stranger might be one of  the two blue wizards but it seems odd to  introduce one with such fanfare and not  the other all things considered it's not  particularly plausible that the stranger  is one of these starry  so who is he well you might want to  consider a character you won't find  elsewhere on this list someone who is  guaranteed to play a major role in the  series one of the most famous aspects of  sauron's story during the second age is  that he comes to the elves disguised in  a form so fair that few suspect his true  identity what better way of delivering a  truly shocking twist than to make the  audience believe they're watching a  kindly wizard befriend a humble harfoot  when they are in fact witnessing the  return of the new dark lord  nobody expected to see hobbits in the  rings of power mostly because they don't  show up in middle earth's history until  the third age and yet here they are or  at least their ancestors the harfoots  like the hobbits are short and have  hairy feet but that's where the  resemblance ends the harfoots are a  nomadic group still searching to find  their place in the world the shows  featured harfoots nori brandyfoot poppy  proudfellow largo brandyfoot and  marigoldbrandyfoot encounter the  stranger on their travels which changes  their lives forever the series depiction  of this tribe of halflings is almost  entirely lifted from concerning hobbits  the first chapter of the lord of the  rings this prologue explains that in the  so-called wandering days hobbits  inhabited the upper veils of anduin to  the east of the misty mountains  according to tolkien the harfots had  much to do with dwarves in ancient times  and long lived in the foothills of the  mountains only when the fortress of dol  guldur rose in the early third age did  the hobbits migrate westwards eventually  settling in the lands that became the  shire clearly these aren't the simple  merry gardeners we all know and love but  the harford's tale should nevertheless  offer up a tantalizing opportunity to  explore the secret history of one of  middle earth's most remarkable races  i can't help but feel there's  wonders in this world  beyond our wondering  because of the show's condensed timeline  we can assume the movers and shakers of  late numenorean history will be present  from early on including isildur son of  ellendyl and future king of gondor at  this point in time isildor is heir to  the lordship of endunia a seaport  located on the island's west coast  notably he is not yet king and at the  beginning of the story does not expect  to become one as the days grow darker  and sauron's shadow falls upon numenor  however a steel door will doubtlessly be  drawn into the dark lord's deadly  schemes if you're familiar with the lord  of the rings you know this ends  tragically one report claims he'll  accompany galadriel elendil and the  numenorean queen muriel on a maritime  journey to middle-earth where exactly  are they going and why  your guess is as good as ours  better known to lord of the rings fans  as high king of the dunadine and founder  of both gondor and arnor allendel's rise  to glory and eventual death at the hands  of sauron is sure to be a key storyline  in the rings of power early on though  he's merely a numenorian lord albeit a  powerful one it's rumored that allendale  will head up recruitment efforts and  lead the numenorean fleet on its voyage  to middle-earth tolkien writes in the  appendices to the lord of the rings that  the numenoreans began to settle in  middle-earth around the year sa-1200 a  few hundred years before the forging of  the rings of power  since the series condenses the timeline  to a single generation it makes sense  that season one will see the island  nation spread its influence across  middle earth allendel it appears will  lead that effort as the seasons go by  expect to see him come increasingly into  conflict with far zone king of numenor  as the latter falls under the influence  of the dark lord the past is dead  we either move forward  or we die with it  an epic saga is only as great as its  monsters while sauron will undoubtedly  be doing much of the heavy lifting in  this department in the rings of power  numenorean king farzone is nothing to be  trifled with far zone the golden becomes  king of numenor after forcibly marrying  muriel daughter of tarpon tear the  previous king after sauron conquers much  of middle earth and declares himself  king of men uninfuriated farzone invades  the mainland swiftly and decisively  defeating the dark lord when the  numenoreans take sauron back to their  homeland as a hostage however he quickly  gets to work corrupting them for his own  ends far zone's subsequent fall into  darkness has world changing consequences  it appears that at the beginning of  season 1 farzone is merely a subject of  tar polynei and an advisor to his future  wife muriel the show's far zone is a  brilliant authoritative and  self-important type which suggests the  character's wicked personality is  already fully formed even if his tragic  fate has yet to play out  muriel is the daughter of tarpalantir  the king of numenor and the unwilling  wife to be of far zone tolkien wrote  little about tarmurial aside from the  disturbing nature of her marriage but it  appears that she'll be a significant  part of the numenorean storyline in the  rings of power it appears that muriel  will experience visions of numenor's  eventual destruction it's also reported  that muriel will be a queen during the  events of the show though her father is  still alive it's not yet known why this  might be it's said that she'll be a  savvy intelligent and compassionate  ruler and will see action in at least  one battle after joining the numenorean  expedition to middle-earth it's clear  that the show's version of muriel will  have much more impact on the history of  middle-earth than her literary  counterpart  tarpalantir is unusual among the late  kings of numenor though his immediate  predecessors mistrusted the elves and  the godlike valor he takes a royal name  in the elvish language quenya as well as  the  title tar as opposed to r taken from the  numenorean language this puts him at  odds with his own people who are  provoked by far zone into open rebellion  after tar polynei dies far zone usurps  the throne and marries his daughter  muriel tarpalantir is definitely in the  show though he likely won't be a major  player we currently have no idea who'll  be playing him most probably tarpon tier  will be an elderly ruler with diminished  authority as the series opens his main  purpose will be to open up a power  vacuum allowing the villainous far zone  to ascend to the throne of numenor  the rings of power will feature several  ancillary numenorean characters all of  whom are original to the series the  first is aryan isildur's sister who is  played by emma horvath aryan is set  against isildur's decision to join the  numenorean expedition to middle-earth at  one point she chases him through the  city streets as he leads his soldiers to  war finally there's kevin as far zone's  son kevin will spend season 1 involved  in a romance with aryan kevin is played  by leon waddem but it's unclear what his  role in the larger story of the rings of  power will be as galadriel narrates the  early days of arda she speaks briefly  about the years of trees and the great  wars that dominated the first age  shortly after we meet her brother finrod  feligant as her narration continues it's  revealed that he is one of the great  elven warriors determined to end  morgoth's reign of terror in middle  earth much of the introduction to the  rings of power is condensed likely  because a majority of the story takes  place during the second age but the  particular battle that galadriel  references might be the culmination of  and ultimate end to the first stage  there's a lot to digest here in  tolkien's writing finrod is an elven  king and brother of galadriel he plays a  key role in the events of the first age  and eventually gives his life to save  baron from torture at the hands of  sauron who at that point is merely a  lieutenant of the dark lord morgoth the  battle in question could be the epic  decor bragalok or even a glimpse of  baron's quest for the silmaril  unfortunately most of this material is  found in the silmarillion which means  amazon studios can't use it for the  series luckily the appendices of the  lord of the rings do mention a few key  details about finrod including his  familial relationship with galadriel and  the manner of his death the odds are  that finrod's appearance in the rings of  power will be relegated to the show's  prologue which with some clever trickery  and editing could feasibly depict middle  earth's ancient history without  committing copyright infringement  in may 2022 amazon invited a number of  token fan blogs websites and youtubers  to a special event in london where they  were introduced to the showrunners and  shown footage from the rings of power  information about the event has since  started popping up all over the internet  according to several sources show  creators patrick mckay and john d payne  told the attendees they have plans for  all the characters in the second age  specifically naming kell born and  calibrian as examples these names will  be familiar to fans of the lord of the  rings kelliborn is galadriel's husband  while calabrian is elrond's ill-fated  wife neither kellebor nor calibrian will  be appearing in season one for a long  time their absence from the cast list  was pretty conspicuous now though we  know that these two characters will  simply be making their debut at a later  point this makes total sense considering  how many characters are due to appear in  the series it stands to reason that a  few may need to be kept off the board  for a while and if nothing else it's  greatly reassuring to know that the  storytellers behind the rings of power  know exactly where their tail is headed  [Music]
 

